Q: Why do we use ServletInitializer (SpringBootServletInitializer)?

One line answer:
So a Spring Boot app packaged as a WAR can start inside an external server (Tomcat/WildFly) — without it,
only `main()` / embedded JAR style works.

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Why it exists

Default Spring Boot:
- You run `main()` → `SpringApplication.run(...)`
- Embedded Tomcat starts with the JAR

But sometimes production says:
- Deploy WAR on external Tomcat / WildFly / WebLogic
- Server owns the lifecycle (not your `main()`)

Problem:
External servlet container does NOT call your `main()` method.
It looks for a Servlet 3.0+ initializer to boot the web app.

Solution:
Extend `SpringBootServletInitializer` and override `configure(...)`.
Container calls this → Spring Boot application context starts.

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ What ServletInitializer does

`SpringBootServletInitializer` bridges:
External Servlet Container  →  Spring Boot Application

It creates a `SpringApplicationBuilder`, registers your `@SpringBootApplication` class,
and starts the Spring context when the WAR is deployed.

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Minimal example

@SpringBootApplication
public class MyApp extends SpringBootServletInitializer {

    // Used when deployed as WAR on external server
    @Override
    protected SpringApplicationBuilder configure(SpringApplicationBuilder builder) {
        return builder.sources(MyApp.class);
    }

    // Used when you run locally as JAR / IDE
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(MyApp.class, args);
    }
}

Also usually:
- `<packaging>war</packaging>`
- embedded Tomcat scope `provided` (or excluded)
- servlet API `provided`

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ When do you NEED it?

| Scenario                              | Need SpringBootServletInitializer? |
| ------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |
| Run as JAR with embedded Tomcat       | No                                 |
| Deploy WAR to external Tomcat/WildFly | Yes                                |
| Spring Boot + Docker fat JAR          | No                                 |
| Traditional company server (WAR only) | Yes                                |

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Interview Q&A

Q: Can I deploy a Spring Boot WAR without ServletInitializer?
A: Not reliably for Servlet 3.x programmatic boot — container won’t start your Spring Boot app via `main()`.

Q: Does ServletInitializer replace main()?
A: No. Keep both: `main()` for local/JAR, `configure()` for external WAR deploy.

Q: Is this the same as web.xml?
A: Related idea (app startup hook). Servlet 3.0+ can boot without web.xml using `WebApplicationInitializer`; SpringBootServletInitializer is Spring Boot’s implementation of that bridge.

